The case describes a young infant (ex-preterm with BPD) who presents with a short prodrome of coryza followed by progressive respiratory distress, wheezing, tachypnea, retractions, feeding difficulty and hypoxemia. This is the classic clinical picture of acute viral bronchiolitis.

Evidence-based management of bronchiolitis is strictly supportive:
- correction of hypoxemia and work of breathing (oxygen and, when necessary, non-invasive or invasive ventilatory support);
- maintenance of hydration (intravenous fluids when oral intake is inadequate).

All other listed interventions are not indicated as initial therapy:
- antibiotics (A) have no role in uncomplicated viral bronchiolitis;
- bronchodilators and systemic corticosteroids (C) lack consistent benefit and are not recommended routinely;
- chest physiotherapy and intramuscular dexamethasone (D) are ineffective or potentially harmful.

Thus the only appropriate initial approach among the options is intravenous hydration plus ventilatory support.

